Axillary discomfort and mild epidermal chaffing from short-term crutch use
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Axillary erythema, Friction-induced desquamation, Tenderness corresponding to crutch placement

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-001
Reason for Visit: Acute localized pain and skin breakdown in the axilla requiring prompt assessment to rule out early cellulitis or deep tissue injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute localized pain and evolving epidermal desquamation with risk of superimposed bacterial infection. Urgent physical assessment was required to assess for deep soft tissue induration or cellulitis prior to the next in-hours period to prevent deterioration and need for systemic antibiotics.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The skin under my arm is burning and peeling away.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with localized burning pain in the axillary vault evolving over the past 48 hours following short-term crutch use. Erythematous macules have developed on the medial upper arm and lateral chest wall. Reports superficial epidermal desquamation in friction areas and transient stinging exacerbated by sweat. Notes mild tenderness to palpation over the axillary skin. Denies swollen glands or systemic symptoms. Patient confirms normal active range of motion of the shoulder. No signs of deep tissue induration.
Functional Status: Ambulating with crutches, independent in ADLs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Temporary mobility impairment, potential for progressive axillary nerve compression if crutch use technique is not corrected.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/78 | HR: 82 | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, mild discomfort with upper extremity movement.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals well-demarcated area of irritation corresponding exactly to crutch placement. Notable for axillary erythema, friction-induced desquamation, and tenderness corresponding to crutch placement. Absence of regional lymphadenopathy. Normal active range of motion of the shoulder. Normal physical exam of deep musculoskeletal structures with no specific imaging required.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathways, appropriate lighting, crutches stored nearby.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current topical agents or analgesics used.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Axillary discomfort and mild epidermal chaffing from short-term crutch use - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Allergic contact dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Exact correspondence to mechanical friction zones and absence of classic vesicular rash.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of spreading diffuse erythema, lack of deep tissue induration, and normal temperature.

Axillary hidradenitis suppurativa - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute onset linked to mechanical trauma, absence of deep subcutaneous nodules or sinus tracts.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Presentation involves acute skin breakdown and burning pain in an area of high mechanical friction and moisture. Given the localized nature of the desquamation and the risk of rapid progression to secondary bacterial infection or axillary nerve neuropraxia with continued incorrect crutch use, a home assessment was essential. The evaluation could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period, as failure to intervene promptly and correct the mechanical cause would lead to predictable health deterioration.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Application of barrier cream containing zinc oxide. Recommendation for simple oral analgesia as needed.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply barrier cream before crutch use to reduce friction and pain. Adjust crutch height two finger-widths below the axilla to prevent nerve compression and allow healing of current abrasions.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Re-fitting of current crutches.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days if skin breakdown fails to re-epithelialize.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if developing a spreading red rash extending down the arm, severe unremitting pain, numbness or weakness in the hands or fingers, or high fever with chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical based on current presentation; skin integrity can change rapidly.
Clinical Course: Expected full resolution within 5 to 7 days with appropriate barrier protection and correction of crutch sizing.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if redness spreads rapidly, purulent discharge develops, or if numbness in the fingers occurs.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Barrier creams reduce shearing forces on the damaged epidermis allowing the skin barrier to regenerate. Correcting crutch height removes the mechanical cause of the injury.
Actionable Steps: Re-measure crutch height immediately. Apply barrier cream twice daily.

